<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rollo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 860–c. 930</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rollo was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader of Scandinavian origin who founded the Norman principality on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>. After a failed attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> in 911, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> granted him lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, traditionally by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-clair-sur-epte">Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</a>, in return for defending the river and accepting baptism. His descendants, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, ruled Normandy as counts and dukes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rollo.jpg" alt="Rollo" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RolloA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rollo, in Old Norse Hrólfr, was almost certainly born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, in either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, but his origin is heavily disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dudo-of-saint-quentin">Dudo of Saint-Quentin</a>, writing in the late 10th century at the request of Rollo's grandson Richard I of Normandy, called him Danish and said he left after a hostile Danish king killed his brother Gurim. Medieval Norwegian and Icelandic writers identified him with Hrólfr the Walker, son of the jarl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rognvald-eysteinsson">Rognvald Eysteinsson</a> of Møre in western Norway.</p><p>The debate was begun in the late nineteenth century by the Danish historian Johannes Steenstrup and partly followed national lines. British, French and Norwegian scholars have generally accepted the Norwegian-Icelandic tradition, while Danish, Swedish and German historians have tended to follow Dudo. The question cannot be settled conclusively. Dudo's work was an official biography, and many of his genealogical claims may have been invented to legitimise Rollo's line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The earliest well-attested event associated with Rollo is the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 885 and 886, in which he was a prominent leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, fended off by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-of-france">Odo of France</a>. In 911 Robert I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> defeated another Viking band at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> with his horsemen, which made possible Rollo's baptism and settlement. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-clair-sur-epte">Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</a>, Charles III recognised the lands Rollo held in return for baptism and help in defending the realm. Rollo took the baptismal name Robert after his godfather, Robert I, and the agreement was to be sealed by his marriage to Gisela, Charles's daughter.</p><p>After his oath Rollo divided the lands between the Epte and the Risle among his chieftains and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> his capital. The earliest record of him is a charter of 918 mentioning Rollo and his associates, and a later agreement under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolph-of-france">Rudolph of France</a> conceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessin">Bessin</a> and Maine to a group of Norsemen presumed to be his. He died between a last mention in 928 and 933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His son and successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-longsword">William Longsword</a> and his grandson Richard the Fearless forged Normandy into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>'s most cohesive principality. The descendants of Rollo and his men assimilated with Frankish culture and became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>. Rollo was the great-great-great-grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, and his direct male descendants ruled Normandy until Stephen of Blois became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Duke of Normandy in 1135.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rollo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scandinavian raiders had attacked the river valley since the 840s, and by the early tenth century a band led by <b>Rollo</b> had established itself around Rouen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 911 Charles the Simple granted the leader <b>Rollo</b> lands around Rouen, the origin of the Duchy of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>William Longsword, son of <b>Rollo</b> and ruler of Normandy since about 927, was ambushed and killed by men of Arnulf I, Count of Flanders, at a peace conference at Picquigny.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rollo</b>'s Vikings, who had besieged Chartres in West Francia since spring, were defeated when a relief army arrived.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rognvald's family spread overseas: his brother Sigurd became the first Earl of Orkney, his son Torf-Einarr later ruled there, and Icelandic saga tradition made <b>Rollo</b>, founder of Normandy, another son.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-more">Earldom of Møre</a></li><li><blockquote>In 911 Charles the Simple granted <b>Rollo</b> the land that became the Duchy of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-francia">West Francia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Normandy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=905" aria-label="Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 155 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rollo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=905">What was here in 905 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
